\python sets the language of the current pane to Python.
It takes no arguments:

\python

After this, every line executed in this pane is sent to
the Python interpreter instead of the LiveLily parser.
The first time \python is called, it also starts the Pyo
audio server and the audio stream, using the number of
channels set with \inchannels and \outchannels. Set these
before calling \python, as the server is started only
once per session.

In a Python pane, a line that starts with "main " is
executed in the main thread, for example:

    main print("hello")

If Python code prints a line that starts with "livelily: ",
the rest of the printed text is typed into the pane, and
with "livelily-2: " it is typed into pane 2. See \pystr
for how this text is typed.

In a Python pane, help(name) opens the documentation of
name in the pane, as \help does in LiveLily, for example:

    help(Sine)

For an object, as a of a = Sine(), it opens the
documentation of its class. The pane's text comes back
with Ctrl+w in insert mode, or :q in normal mode.

From a LiveLily pane you can send a line of code to the
Python interpreter without switching language, with the
send method:

\python.send a.stop()

This works only after \python has been called once,
otherwise you get the error "python has not been
initialized". Any other method gives an error.
To switch the pane back, use \livelily or \lua.
